{"id":"e14t2","name":"User Browsing Profiles","lang":"pyspark","entry":"solve","params":["views_df"],"limit_s":30,"ordered":true,"desc":"<p>The recommendation team wants a compact profile per user, built from product-page views: which categories the user looked at, and in which order.</p>\n<p>Implement <code>solve</code> so that it returns one row per user.</p>\n<h3>Input</h3>\n<p><code>views_df</code></p>\n<table class=\"mdt\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>column</th>\n<th>type</th>\n<th>description</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>user_id</td>\n<td>string</td>\n<td>never null</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>viewed_at</td>\n<td>string</td>\n<td>format <code>yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss</code>; may be null</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>category</td>\n<td>string</td>\n<td>free text with inconsistent case and spaces; may be null</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Rules</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><code>category</code> is normalized by trimming leading and trailing spaces and converting to lowercase (spaces inside the value are kept).</li>\n<li>Views whose normalized <code>category</code> is null or empty, or whose <code>viewed_at</code> is null, are ignored. Users without any remaining view do not appear in the output.</li>\n<li><code>num_views</code> is the number of remaining views of the user.</li>\n<li><code>categories</code> is the list of distinct categories of the user, sorted alphabetically.</li>\n<li><code>journey</code> is the list of the categories of all remaining views of the user (duplicates kept), in chronological order of <code>viewed_at</code>. Views with the same <code>viewed_at</code> are ordered by category alphabetically.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Output</h3>\n<p>A DataFrame with exactly these columns, in this order:</p>\n<table class=\"mdt\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>column</th>\n<th>type</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>user_id</td>\n<td>string</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>num_views</td>\n<td>long</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>categories</td>\n<td>array<string></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>journey</td>\n<td>array<string></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Rows are sorted by <code>user_id</code> ascending.</p>\n<h3>Example</h3>\n<p><code>views_df</code></p>\n<table class=\"mdt\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>user_id</th>\n<th>viewed_at</th>\n<th>category</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>u1</td>\n<td>2024-03-01 10:05:00</td>\n<td>\"Shoes\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>u1</td>\n<td>2024-03-01 10:00:00</td>\n<td>\" books \"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>u1</td>\n<td>2024-03-01 10:10:00</td>\n<td>\"shoes\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>u1</td>\n<td>2024-03-01 10:07:00</td>\n<td>null</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>u2</td>\n<td>2024-03-02 09:00:00</td>\n<td>\"Toys\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>u2</td>\n<td>2024-03-02 08:00:00</td>\n<td>\"toys\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>u3</td>\n<td>2024-03-02 08:00:00</td>\n<td>\"  \"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>u1</td>\n<td>2024-03-01 10:20:00</td>\n<td>\"Garden\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Expected output</p>\n<table class=\"mdt\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>user_id</th>\n<th>num_views</th>\n<th>categories</th>\n<th>journey</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>u1</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>[books, garden, shoes]</td>\n<td>[books, shoes, shoes, garden]</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>u2</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>[toys]</td>\n<td>[toys, toys]</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Function signature</h3>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from pyspark.sql import DataFrame\n\ndef solve(views_df: DataFrame) -&gt; DataFrame:\n    ...\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Constraints</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Use the PySpark DataFrame API. Do not convert to pandas, do not call <code>collect()</code> or <code>toPandas()</code> inside <code>solve</code>, and do not use Python UDFs.</li>\n<li><code>views_df</code> has up to 10^8 rows; a single user has at most 10^4 views.</li>\n<li>The returned DataFrame may be empty; its columns and types must still match the specification.</li>\n</ul><div class=\"lim\"><b>[execution time limit]</b> 30 seconds (pyspark)<br><b>[memory limit]</b> 2 GB</div>","starter":"from pyspark.sql i